A simulation tries at most twelve of a triggered ability's options (Commander Duels v0.9.8). Every
format.

Reason: the campaign's court ran the engine out of memory (v0.9.7 measured it: one court game in
six on a 10 GB heap, and every game's peak at or over the limit). A histogram of the live objects
and a thread dump showed what filled it. When a triggered ability triggers in a simulation and has
more than one way to choose its targets or modes, SimulatedPlayer2.triggerAbility copies the game
for every one of them, all before the search scores the first, and adds each copy to the search
tree. A trigger with two targets among thirty permanents is 870 copies: at the court one search
held near 900 copies of the game alive, 3 GB of a 3 GB heap, and the same game, seed 14525, died at
turn 39 here and on GitHub. The loop does not look for the search's interruption either, so a
search told to stop (its budget, or since v0.9.8 a filling heap) went on copying for seconds and
was abandoned still holding its copies. The real game never takes a trigger's targets from the
simulation (XMage's own TODO there says so): the simulation only scores the line that sets the
trigger off, and a sample of the options does that. So triggerAbility now tries at most
MAX_TRIGGER_OPTIONS (12) of them, spread evenly over the list in its order, and stops between
copies when the search is interrupted. With twelve options or fewer nothing changes. The same
court game then finishes, holding at most 38 copies of the game at once.
The other fan-out of this kind, a search effect's cards (ComputerPlayer6.resolve), is bounded in
Commander Duels' own FairBot, which overrides it.
Upstream: none.
Remove when: XMage's simulation bounds the options it tries for a triggered ability.

